WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.950
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today, Congressman Ro Khanna, representing

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:11.540
California's 17th district, which includes
Silicon Valley, joining us today from a car

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.540
very much.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.540
Great.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.540
Great to have you back on.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.930
Speaker 6: David Pakman are always, of course,
always safe.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.580
Speaker 1: So, OK.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:27.170
Supply chain shortages, gas prices, a charge
squeeze from China that are expected to be

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.170
Christmas gifts.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:33.750
You know, there's this there's this broad
range of what is milk cost, what do toys cost,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:34.780
et cetera.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:41.530
Initially, there was the idea that this is
a demand resurgence after there was a demand

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.719
evaporation during the pandemic.

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:47.280
It's now pretty clear that it's more than
that.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.430
But what's your kind of general assessment
of what we're seeing right now?

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.500
Speaker 6: Well, we're seeing inflation, and
this is why we need two things we need to

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.859
pass bill back better, which would put more
money in the pockets of working families so

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:02.969
that they can pay for their groceries and
gas.

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:05.550
It would lower prescription drug prices.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:11.940
It would deal with some of the supply chain
issues by investing money in our ports, by

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.570
providing an incentive for people to return,
to return to work.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.690
And so we need to pass it.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:28.310
And then the said, you know, as Larry Summers
has said, the Fed needs to make sure that

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:34.500
they stop, in my view, the the mortgage buybacks
and stop increasing the money supply that's

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.320
largely on the Fed and they can take that
action.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.340
Speaker 1: Let's pick one item, which is gas
prices, because it gets so much attention.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:48.790
You know, for years, I have not participated
in the you either blame or praise the president

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.420
for what's going on, but gas prices have been.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:57.920
My best assessment is if a president wants,
they can tap into the strategic reserve, which

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.470
will eventually lower gas prices maybe 18
to 20 cents a gallon on what might be a temporary

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.470
basis.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:10.780
I mean, it seems really that presidents don't
have very much to do with gas prices, but

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.610
yet it's a very popular political football
to throw around, isn't it?

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:19.340
Speaker 6: Well, this president, I think,
did the right thing by having tapping the

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.100
Strategic Petroleum Reserve 18 cents per gallon
is significant.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.990
I mean, that means a lot to folks or who are
struggling.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:34.780
But in terms of dramatically lowering prices,
it has more to do with the oil companies.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.780
I mean, they didn't when when oil was 20 to
30 bucks a barrel, they didn't invest now

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.010
that it's much higher there.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.080
They want to reap the benefits.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.160
And so the long term solution to this is price
stability, which is more electric vehicles,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.880
more renewable energy so that we're not dictating,
we're not at the mercy of the vagaries of

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.790
the price of oil.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.060
But in the short term, I think President Biden
is taking the right action.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:06.040
Speaker 1: When you when we look at some of
some other things, like, for example, used

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.300
cars have been a big area of price increase,
starting with the pandemic and continuing

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.930
through building materials is another one,
although it seems that lumber is sort of coming

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.340
back down.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:24.390
How involved do you believe a government should
be on individual areas like these in trying

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.990
to influence price?

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:30.060
Speaker 6: As involved as we can be, I mean,
we have to be realistic that the governments

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.840
usually don't have that many tools to fight
inflation.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:40.090
I mean, it goes back to Gerald Ford with wind
and whip inflation and you know, it's very

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.870
hard to do, but we should do what we can.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.690
In this case, I think the three things we
need to do is put more money in the pockets

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:51.530
of working families so that they have money
to deal with the higher prices.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:56.660
Make sure we're dealing with supply chain
issues at the port and make sure we're incentivizing

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:02.440
more people to work so that you can deal with
some of these supply issues and lower prices.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.069
I guess I'd add one more thing, which is if
there's concentrated industry making sure

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:12.590
people aren't charging prices above the competitive
price and putting pressure on those industries

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:13.930
to charge a fair price.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.870
Speaker 2: There is,
Speaker 1: I guess we would call it, plausible.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.191
Maybe we don't have to say probable yet that
Republicans may well take control of the House

00:04:22.191 --> 00:04:28.380
of Representatives back next year and would
assume control in January of 2023, given that

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.980
we don't know at least what will happen after
next November.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:37.680
What do you believe realistically can get
done in terms of major legislation in the

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.100
next year that that would pass not only the
House, but actually the Senate and become

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.100
law?

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:48.820
Speaker 6: Well, first of all, we need to
make sure we win or it's devastating for our

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:49.820
democracy.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.161
I mean, to have people like Marjorie Taylor
Greene and others in charge after the House

00:04:55.161 --> 00:05:01.430
of Representatives is a fate that really no
one, in my view in the Democratic side, should,

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.820
should want and shouldn't take, should take
very seriously.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.030
I think we have to obviously, we have to pass
build back better with a lower prescription

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:13.320
drug costs, lower child care costs, lower
elder care costs.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.419
We need to, in my view, deliver on the $15
minimum wage.

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:19.490
The president can unilaterally forgive student
loans.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.410
He ought to do that.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.600
Have some forgiveness of student loans.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.600
Voting rights.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.130
We have to deliver voting rights.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.110
This is our chance to do it.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:33.370
Or we're basically stuck with state voter
suppression laws and we need to make sure

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.540
that we're tackling immigration.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:40.210
I mean, if it doesn't make it into build back
better, there has to be some delivering on

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:41.210
immigration.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.340
So those would be some of the things I think
that are the priorities for next year

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.000
Speaker 1: on on student loans.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.540
I got a lot of emails from people who say,
I'm really mad that Joe Biden is not moving

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:53.010
more quickly, unforgiving some student loan
debt.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:58.210
I came into this presidency not believing
that Joe Biden had any intention of doing

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.210
that.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.210
I'm not.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.160
I'm not praising or saying I'm mad at him.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.750
I just never thought that this was a priority
for Joe Biden.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.889
I didn't think he was going to forgive any
student debt.

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:10.900
Is that your view or do you think that that's
something he might be able to be pressured

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:11.900
to do?

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.690
Speaker 6: I think you should be persuaded
to do it, I think it can help really build

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:21.479
support in with him, with young people around
the country, and it's the right thing to do,

00:06:21.479 --> 00:06:26.890
and he understands that we're being married
to the first lady who was an educator, how

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:30.210
much debt we're saddling the younger generation
with now.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:35.840
I don't think he's got to do 50000 as Senator
Warren, Senator Schumer and I and others have

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.840
asked.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.200
But could he do 10 20000 and would that materially
make a difference in people's lives?

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.200
Absolutely.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.950
And I think it would be one of the biggest
things he could do to build momentum for his

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:48.950
presidency.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.130
Speaker 1: You mentioned Marjorie Taylor Greene,
as some in my audience know.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:58.180
The House voted to strip her of her committee
assignments some months ago.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:04.130
Your colleague Ayanna Pressley has now suggested
proposed doing the same with with Lauren Boebert

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:09.169
over her multiple recent videos related to
stories about being in elevators with Ilhan

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:12.889
Omar and the jihad squad, et cetera.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:14.660
What's your view about doing that?

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:18.870
Because it's obviously correct on the merits,
and I would go further that these folks have

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.500
no business even being in the House of Representatives.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:27.150
But is it not a pyrrhic victory in some sense,
where they end up saying I've been canceled,

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.820
donate money and it gives them attention?

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.030
Speaker 6: Well, David, you're actually right.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:35.050
I was talking to a friend of mine on the Republican
side yesterday and he said, When are you going

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:40.759
to kick me off my committee so I can go raise
a million bucks an email to my my list?

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:45.110
So this is why I think the speaker is being
judicious about this discussing it.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.860
Obviously, we have to take action.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.210
It has to be clear that that kind of racism,
that kind of Islamophobia has no place.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.610
But we don't want to make.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:58.789
Representative Barbara Martyr and do something
that's going just further fuel her platform.

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:02.880
And that's why I think the speaker's take
your time consulting the caucus.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.900
I will support whatever action she ends up
taking.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.979
We do need to take some action behind.

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:13.949
Speaker 1: Sometimes we learn that there are
interesting political bedfellows behind the

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:19.380
scenes where publicly there's a lot of conflict
and their friends behind the scenes.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:25.200
But right now, with some of these personalities
that exist, for example, this rift between

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.639
Congresswoman Boebert and Ilhan Omar.

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:33.059
Is there more palpable tension behind the
scenes where it is not nearly as collegial

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:34.259
as it tends to be?

00:08:34.259 --> 00:08:39.539
Speaker 6: I think on some of the fringes,
yes, there are a few people who probably you

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:43.589
don't interact with, you don't even sometimes
say hi to.

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:45.790
But not among the vast majority.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:50.179
I mean, I still have good relations on the
other side.

00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:52.519
I was speaking, I speak often.

00:08:52.519 --> 00:08:57.550
Representative Kamar, we disagree strongly
on issues of oil and gas, other issues.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:00.940
But he's always engages in good faith.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.920
There are, you know, dozens of members on
the other side who I still work with.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.629
You have to get legislation through.

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:11.880
Speaker 1: I want to talk a little bit about
the general approach that you think the Democratic

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.930
Party should take going into the 2022 election.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.360
One of the things that makes it increasingly
tough to run against the kind of modern Republicans

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.089
of the Trump and post-Trump era is, for the
most part, they're not even pretending to

00:09:24.089 --> 00:09:25.720
be about policy anymore.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:26.769
They're about obstruction.

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:31.139
They're about cultural issues like critical
race theory or a Christmas tree was burned

00:09:31.139 --> 00:09:34.430
and it's part of the war on Christmas or whatever
the case may be.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.689
So that's very silly, but it also makes it
sort of tough to run against them because

00:09:38.689 --> 00:09:44.069
if they're not arguing on policy, it's hard
to fight against things like the left wants

00:09:44.069 --> 00:09:48.930
to turn this country into a socialist country
or these kind of vague statements.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.990
So how how do you run against that?

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:56.680
I'm not convinced that just continuing to
talk about policy like a train is actually

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:57.829
is is what's effective.

00:09:57.829 --> 00:10:03.360
Speaker 6: Well, we lead with patriotism,
we talked about how we have an aspirational

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.519
vision for America.

00:10:04.519 --> 00:10:08.509
We have a vision of an America where we actually
win the 21st century.

00:10:08.509 --> 00:10:09.660
We're proud of being Americans.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.189
Everyone should be taught to be proud of being
Americans, that what we want to make sure

00:10:13.189 --> 00:10:16.740
is people shouldn't have to be forced to leave
their hometowns.

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:23.899
We need to bring prosperity and modern opportunity
to places that have long been left out so

00:10:23.899 --> 00:10:26.179
that they can have dignity so they can stay.

00:10:26.179 --> 00:10:30.179
Kids can stay with their families and that,
you know, Trump promised all these things,

00:10:30.179 --> 00:10:35.839
but he hasn't delivered and and we will build
a strong, vibrant, great America.

00:10:35.839 --> 00:10:40.440
I think we don't emphasize the patriotism
enough, the aspiration enough.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:45.050
You have to get to people's hearts to get
them to think, yeah, they really have a vision

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:46.970
that they care about this country.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.149
They care about my community.

00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:53.999
And then you can get into the specifics, but
you can't lead with the specifics without.

00:10:53.999 --> 00:10:55.269
What is the ED?

00:10:55.269 --> 00:10:56.800
What is what is the purpose?

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.059
Human beings aren't just economic actors.

00:10:59.059 --> 00:11:05.019
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's what seems extraordinarily
difficult about the situation, which is I

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:11.110
feel very strongly that when there is a policy
debate, we win the policy debate very clearly.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:17.600
But if the if the circumstances don't allow
you to be speaking to a public that is expecting

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:24.100
to have the policy debate, and instead they
want the debate over who is socialist or patriotic

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.060
or whatever the case may be, it feels like
a losing battle in some sense.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:33.460
Speaker 6: Well, we can win that debate that
our values are the true American values, our

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:40.129
values of freedom, of ideas and expression,
of respect for people of all faiths, of a

00:11:40.129 --> 00:11:45.079
respect for a community of civility and decency.

00:11:45.079 --> 00:11:49.410
These are the American values that we're standing
up for and the fulfillment of those values

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.550
and a spreading opportunity and prosperity.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:58.899
I think we ought to frame them as we're standing
up for the deepest American values, the fundamental

00:11:58.899 --> 00:12:00.089
American values.

00:12:00.089 --> 00:12:05.559
And not that we're here's our five point plan
just to put more money in your pocket.

00:12:05.559 --> 00:12:06.769
That matters.

00:12:06.769 --> 00:12:08.939
But people care holistically.

00:12:08.939 --> 00:12:14.300
They want to see our nation thrive and succeed,
and they want to know what their places in

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.600
this nation.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.459
And we have to speak to that deeper aspiration
that people have and people are very much

00:12:20.459 --> 00:12:21.730
vote on identity.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:27.180
Speaker 1: Before I let you go, I'm curious
now in thinking about 2022.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:32.160
Of course, every member of the house is up
for reelection many and very safe seats, so

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:37.420
the number of truly contested seats is, of
course, only a fraction of the total one-third

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.869
of the Senate is, of course, up.

00:12:38.869 --> 00:12:41.470
There's a number of governors races, et cetera.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:47.699
How much talk behind the scenes is there about
the role that the former president Donald

00:12:47.699 --> 00:12:53.420
Trump is going to try to play either in general,
in the midterms or in in specific races?

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.100
And does it really matter in terms of the
strategy for Democrats?

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.230
Speaker 6: I do think he's going to play a
role he's already playing a role in Georgia,

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:05.699
challenging an incumbent governor who's challenging
some sitting House members.

00:13:05.699 --> 00:13:11.649
I think it provides an opportunity for Democrats
when the Republican Party is so divided and

00:13:11.649 --> 00:13:17.160
they're still having people who are denying
Joe Biden's a legitimate election running

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.839
for office on that platform.

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:20.980
But I don't think it's sufficient for Democrats.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:27.769
I think it gives us an opportunity to compete
to not be written off, to maintain our majority.

00:13:27.769 --> 00:13:32.850
But we have to have a vision that delivers
for people and that convinces them that we

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:38.269
really are speaking to what what matters to
them and manage them, not just economically

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:41.379
matters to their conception of our country.

00:13:41.379 --> 00:13:43.999
And only if we do that can we can we keep
the house.

00:13:43.999 --> 00:13:48.999
Speaker 1: Do you think the Virginia gubernatorial
from last month was a warning about making

00:13:48.999 --> 00:13:51.759
it too much about running against Trumpism?

00:13:51.759 --> 00:13:57.119
Because Young can seem to kind of ride the
fence well enough that it was not a good strategy?

00:13:57.119 --> 00:14:01.100
Speaker 6: Yeah, I don't think it should be
all about Trump, I think you can be also about

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:05.290
some of the extremes in Congress basically
said, Look, do you want some of these people

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.290
to be cheering?

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:10.439
The next committee isn't to be in charge of
the the new Congress, but then I think it

00:14:10.439 --> 00:14:14.279
has to be a clear vision on our side of what
we have delivered and what we're going to

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:16.860
continue to deliver what we stand for.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:22.529
People ultimately need the positive, the affirmative
to vote for someone.

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:23.529
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:28.970
The more I look at next year, the more I'm
of the mindset of hope for the best and prepare

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:29.970
for the worst.

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.189
And hopefully it works out.

00:14:31.189 --> 00:14:32.459
Not, not too poorly.

00:14:32.459 --> 00:14:36.609
Congressman Ro Khanna representing California's
17th District.

00:14:36.609 --> 00:14:37.609
Always appreciate your time.

00:14:37.609 --> 00:14:39.040
Speaker 6: Thank you, David.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.160
I always enjoy being on.

